        "Add a measured mass of magnesium, quickly fit the bung and start the timer.",
        "Record the gas volume at regular time intervals until it stops increasing; repeat for reliability.",
        "Repeat with different acid concentrations, keeping acid volume, magnesium mass and temperature the same; compare mean rates.",
      ],
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why does concentrated acid react faster?",
      "namedItems": [
        {
          "meaning": "Higher concentration means more acid particles in the same volume.",
          "name": "More particles",
        },
        {
          "meaning": "Acid particles collide with the magnesium surface more frequently.",
          "name": "More collisions",
        },
        {
          "meaning": "Successful collisions happen more frequently, so hydrogen is produced more quickly.",
          "name": "Faster reaction",
        },
      ],
      "slide": 7,
      "text": "",
    },
